# The node detail as a column of cards.
#
# The screen this replaced was a hundred and twenty label-and-value rows with dimmed words
# between them, which is a group distinguished from its own rows by colour alone. What a card
# says instead is said by a fill and an edge, and there are three things about doing that in a
# *scrolling* list that a still of the top of the screen cannot show. So this walks down.
#
# Each card is now headed by its symbol in a disc, and the leading slot is answered per card
# rather than per screen: the one row that carries a symbol is the Actions row at the top, a card
# of its own above the first heading, and the cards of facts under it carry none. Watch that every
# row of a card starts its words in one column even so. The verbs that row opens are their own
# screen - node-actions.scene.
#
# Echo Repeater is the node worth opening here because it reports nearly everything: a traced
# route in both directions, identity, signal, an environment reading with a bar under it, a
# two-channel power monitor and both neighbour groups, which is seven cards and a cut one.

scene demo
tab nodes
# The Nodes list has four rows before its first node - the filter row, the sort row, the Map and
# Waypoints (MESH_UI_NODES_LEAD_ROWS) - so a node's row is its index in the demo roster plus
# four. Echo Repeater is seeds[5] and row 9.
key down 10
key a
hold 1200

# One card in. The cursor on a card of facts focuses the whole card rather than a row - a fact is
# not a control - which is the ring rather than the fill.
key right
hold 1200

# Down through the groups. What to watch is the card the window is cutting: it keeps square
# corners and its full width on the cut end, because a rounded corner halfway down a scroll is a
# card claiming to end where the panel merely stopped.
key down 8
hold 800
key down 8
hold 800
key down 8
hold 1600

# And back up, so the cut lands on the other end of a card as well.
key up 12
hold 1600
